"Localized inhibition in the Drosophila mushroom body | eLife (https://elifesciences.org/articles/56954)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view1\",\"pattern\":\"50 μm\"}); Total lines: 1966\nNo matching text found for \"50 μm\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nChecking your browser - reCAPTCHA (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9613607/)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn3view2\",\"pattern\":\"weak\"}); Total lines: 3\nNo matching text found for \"weak\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Drosophila Whole-Connectome Network Can Learn Human-Designed Cognitive Tasks (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.10014v1)\nciteturn4view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"pattern\":\"Adam\"}); Total lines: 435\nNo matching text found for \"Adam\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Drosophila Whole-Connectome Network Can Learn Human-Designed Cognitive Tasks (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.10014v1)\nciteturn4view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"pattern\":\"Yu\"}); Total lines: 435\nL26:   5. 【16†4 Results】\nL27:     1. 【17†4.1 The connectome topology improves held-out addition】\nL28:     2. 【18†4.2 The topology advantage extends to grounded relational language】\nL29:     3. 【19†4.3 Sensory alignment and relational generalization】\nL30:   6. 【20†5 Discussion】\nL31:   7. 【21†6 Limitations】\nL32:   8. 【22†7 Conclusion】\nL33:   9. 【23†References】\nL34:   10. 【24†A Data and Graph Provenance】\nL35:   11. 【25†B Task, Model, and Training Details】\nL36:     1. 【26†B.1 Shared recurrent parameterization】\nL37:     2. 【27†B.2 Addition task and optimization】\nL38:     3. 【28†B.3 Language task specification】\nL39:     4. 【29†B.4 Corpus generation and split construction】\nL40:     5. 【30†B.5 Language interface and optimization】\nL41:     6. 【31†B.6 Hyperparameter selection and interfaces】\nL42:     7. 【32†B.7 Language controls】\nL43:   12. 【33†C Evaluation Metrics and Statistical Procedures】\nL44:     1. 【34†C.1 Strict paired language endpoint】\nL45:     2. 【35†C.2 Factorial uncertainty and graph ranking】\nL46:   13. 【36†D Rewire Construction and Structural Diagnostics】\nL47:   14. 【37†E Compute and Reproducibility】\nL48:     1. 【38†Code and data availability.】\nL49:   15. 【39†F Detailed Numerical Results and Controls】\nL50:     1. 【40†F.1 Addition runs】\nL51:     2. 【41†F.2 Language factorial and controls】\nL52: 【42†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org】\nL53: \nL54: arXiv:2610.10014v1 [cs.LG] 07 Oct 2026\nL55: # A Drosophila Whole-Connectome Network Can Learn\nL56: Human-Designed Cognitive Tasks\nL57: \nL58: Joonghui Cho     Minchan Kang     Daeshik Kim^{∗}\nL59: \nL60: Korea Advanced Institute of Science and Technology (KAIST)\nL61: \nL62: paul26@kaist.ac.kr  mc.kang@kaist.ac.kr  daeshik@kaist.ac.kr\nL63: \nL64: ^{∗}Corresponding author.\nL65: ###### Abstract\nL66: Can a biological wiring diagram serve as a useful computational substrate beyond the behaviors for which it evolved? We use the publicly released MaleCNS v1.0 connectome, reconstructed from a single adult male Drosophila specimen, as the fixed recurrent topology of an artificial network. We train separate models for bounded addition and for a controlled grounded relational language task built from a fixed 100-word lexicon. In both models, one scalar is learned per anatomical edge.\nL67: The anatomical graph reaches $92.77\\%$ mean accuracy on held-out addition, compared with $67.93\\%$ for directed degree-preserving rewires. On the strict paired language endpoint, which matches original and order-reversed scenes to their corresponding descriptions, it reaches $61.59\\%$ across four fixed interfaces, compared with $44.17\\%$ for matched rewires. At the canonical interface, it ranks first in a fixed 21-graph comparison.\nL68: On the matched 48-group intervention subset, shuffling task-defined sensory features reduces its score from $60.94\\%$ to $19.27\\%$. Together, these results show that higher-order MaleCNS wiring provides a reusable inductive bias for bounded addition and grounded relational language.\nL69: ## 1 Introduction\nL70: \nL71: Connectomes are anatomical records, but they can also be read as candidate architectures. Synapse-resolution reconstructions now cover nearly the entire adult fly brain and the complete adult male central nervous system (CNS), revealing recurrent organizations shaped by an animal’s behavior (【43†Dorkenwald et al., 2024】; 【44†Berg et al., 2026】). We ask whether that organization remains useful when connection strengths are relearned for problems outside the animal’s natural repertoire.\nL72: Most connectome-constrained models have pursued biological prediction in visual and sensorimotor systems, causal-effect estimation, or whole-brain activity fitting (【45†Lappalainen et al., 2024】; 【46†Shiu et al., 2024】; 【47†Pospisil et al., 2024】; 【48†Wang et al., 2026】). Work on artificial tasks is smaller and has adapted fly wiring to image classification, chess, simulated locomotion, and robot navigation (【49†Yu et al., 2026】; 【50†Jin et al., 2026】; 【51†Wang and Chen, 2026】).\nL73: Evidence about the topology itself is mixed. Random structures performed similarly to biological ones in one comparison, and another study found that shared initialization and degree-preserving nulls removed much of an apparent advantage (【52†Pircher et al., 2021】; 【53†Dhiman, 2026】). Task performance by itself does not show whether the biological arrangement of edges matters.\nL74: We use the MaleCNS adjacency as the recurrent mask of an artificial network, with one learned scalar on each retained edge. Addition and grounded relational language are trained as separate models. Addition requires exact answers for unseen operand pairs. The language model receives controlled English alongside task-defined sensory events and must distinguish descriptions of held-out channel pairs and reversed event sequences.\nL75: Orthogonal token codes contain no pretrained lexical semantics, so the mapping must be acquired from the generated corpus and sensory stream.\nL76: The anatomical graph outperforms degree-preserving rewires on both tasks, with the language separation appearing at every tested interface and in an additional 20-rewire ensemble. Sensory shuffling sharply reduces language performance. Figure 【54†1】 introduces the graph, tasks, and main controls.\nL77: 【55†Image: Refer to caption】 Figure 1: Study design. (a) The processed MaleCNS v1.0 adjacency fixes the recurrent support, and each retained directed edge carries one learned scalar. The anatomical rendering shows a deterministic sample of 3,000 neuron skeletons. The overlaid nodes and arrows are schematic. Directed-degree-preserving rewires retain each node’s in- and out-degree. (b) Addition and grounded relational language are separate fits on the same topology.\nL78: Language receives sensory events and fixed token codes and predicts the next symbol through a fixed readout. Held-out evaluation pairs original and order-reversed scenes with their corresponding equal-length descriptions. Orange marks the sensory-shuffle control used only at evaluation.\nL79: ## 2 Related Work\nL80: #### Whole-connectome resources and models.\nL81: FlyWire reconstructed a whole adult female brain with 139,255 neurons and about 50 million chemical synapses (【43†Dorkenwald et al., 2024】). MaleCNS extends synapse-resolution reconstruction across the complete adult male CNS (【44†Berg et al., 2026】). Whole-brain analyses identify rich-club organization and overrepresented recurrent motifs (【56†Lin et al., 2024】).\nL82: Functional models use this anatomical detail to predict visual activity (【45†Lappalainen et al., 2024】), model feeding and grooming transformations (【46†Shiu et al., 2024】), and formulate estimators for causal neural effects (【47†Pospisil et al., 2024】). BrainTrace, for example, applied its online learning method to a FlyWire-constrained spiking model of resting-state regional activity and interregional functional connectivity (【48†Wang et al., 2026】).\nL83: #### Topology as an inductive bias.\nL84: Connectome-constrained reservoir studies have examined time-series prediction and multifunctional dynamics across hyperparameter regimes (【57†Morra et al., 2025】). In small fly-connectome subgraphs, raw task performance was generally comparable to random reservoirs, but weight-cost-normalized performance and robustness to neuron loss or parameter variation were higher (【58†McAllister et al., 2026】).\nL85: Simplified whole-brain communication models likewise produced simulated region-selective activation patterns that were sensitive to degree-preserving rewiring (【59†Zhang et al., 2026】). A recent fly-visual-system study found that advantages over a weak random control largely disappeared with shared from-scratch initialization and degree-preserving nulls (【53†Dhiman, 2026】).\nL86: Artificial-task studies use a complete larval connectome as a reservoir for image classification and chess (【49†Yu et al., 2026】), an adult-brain connectome as a learned controller for simulated fly locomotion (【50†Jin et al., 2026】), and a fly-brain-derived recurrent network for robot navigation (【51†Wang and Chen, 2026】). FlyGM also compares its locomotion controller with a degree-preserving rewire.\nL87: The present experiments move away from embodied behavior to two symbolic mappings and ask whether their performance survives changes in token-input and output placement and graph draw.\nL88: #### Grounding, composition, and arithmetic.\nL89: Fly-inspired language work has used a mathematical mushroom-body motif to learn sparse word representations from text (【60†Liang et al., 2021】). Associations between words and referents can also be learned from paired egocentric visual and linguistic experience (【61†Vong et al., 2024】), and pretrained instruction embeddings can support compositional generalization in sensorimotor networks (【62†Riveland and Pouget, 2024】).\nL90: Benchmarks such as gSCAN, COGS, ReaSCAN, and Winoground test whether familiar components remain interpretable in new combinations or orders (【63†Ruis et al., 2020】; 【64†Kim and Linzen, 2020】; 【65†Wu et al., 2021】; 【66†Thrush et al., 2022】). Our language experiment uses orthogonal token codes, channel-pair holdouts, sensory interventions, and relation reversal for the same general reason.\nL91: The addition experiment reports exact answers and carry-sensitive results, following work that separates predictive performance from mechanistic recovery (【67†Nikankin et al., 2025】).\nL92: ## 3 Methods\nL93: ### 3.1 Experimental design\nL94: The two task models are trained independently on the anatomical topology and on directed degree-preserving rewires. Addition provides a repeated-run comparison across several graphs, whereas language uses a crossed design of five topologies and four input-output interfaces followed by a larger 21-graph comparison at interface I1.\nL95: Rewiring changes the adjacency while retaining node and edge counts, every node’s directed degree, the task interface, parameter count, initialization distribution, optimizer, and training budget.\nL96: ### 3.2 Connectome network and topology controls\nL97: \nL98: We use the public MaleCNS v1.0 release (【44†Berg et al., 2026】) and retain every downloaded neuron assigned a superclass. The processed graph has 166,700 nodes and 25,582,938 directed neuron-to-neuron edges, representing 124,177,617 synaptic contacts. Appendix 【24†A】 gives the specimen, selection, orientation, and provenance details.\nL99: Let $G=(V,E)$ denote the directed graph. For a batch state $h_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{|V|\\times B}$, both task models use a leaky bounded recurrence\nL100: \nL101:  | $$h_{t+1}=(1-\\alpha)h_{t}+\\alpha\\tanh\\!\\left(u_{t}+W_{E}h_{t}\\right),$$  |  | (1)\nL102: where $\\alpha$ is the leak coefficient, $u_{t}$ is the task-specific input drive, and $W_{E}$ has nonzeros only on $E$. For an edge $e=(j\\!\\to\\!i)$, $(W_{E})_{ij}=\\theta_{e}/\\sqrt{\\max(d_{i}^{\\mathrm{in}},1)}$, where $d_{i}^{\\mathrm{in}}$ is the in-degree of node $i$. The recurrent gain is one in both tasks. Synaptic contact counts determine only the mask, and edge scalars are initialized independently.\nL103: The edge vector $\\theta\\in\\mathbb{R}^{|E|}$ is the only trainable tensor, with input and readout maps and codebooks held fixed.\nL104: Rewired controls are generated by directed double-edge swaps that preserve every node’s unweighted in-degree and out-degree, retain the original self-loops, and introduce no duplicate edges. After five accepted swaps per original edge, only $0.588$–$0.594\\%$ of source edges remain across the 20-graph ensemble. The comparison tests the collective contribution of higher-order properties, including reciprocity, motifs, paths, communities, and spectra, beyond directed degree.\nL105: Appendix 【36†D】 gives the construction details and structural diagnostics.\nL106: ### 3.3 Bounded-addition protocol\nL107: The task contains every ordered pair of nonnegative integers satisfying $a+b\\leq 100$. An expression $a+b$ is encoded in 47 fixed channels: two 22-dimensional digit codes and three operator channels. The network receives 64 recurrent updates, and logits for the 101 answers from 0 through 100 are averaged over the final four steps. Training minimizes cross-entropy plus a normalized ordinal CDF loss with unit coefficient.\nL108: The 5,151 expressions are divided into 3,584 training, 775 validation, and 792 test examples. Commuted expressions are grouped before splitting, so no unordered operand pair occurs in more than one split. Each epoch visits all training expressions once without replacement, and the checkpoint with the lowest validation loss is selected before a single evaluation of the test set.\nL109: The comparison contains four optimization runs on the anatomical graph, four on the first rewire, and two each on two additional rewires, yielding an unbalanced graph-by-initialization design. We report the individual runs and their descriptive aggregate.\nL110: ### 3.4 Grounded relational language protocol\nL111: \nL112: The language task pairs sensory events with a question and a controlled English answer. In one held-out example, strong pressure on the right middle leg occurs before weak movement on the right front leg. Asked “What did I feel?”, the target answer begins “The strong pressure on the right middle leg happened before the weak movement on the right front leg.” Table 【68†3】 gives the complete record and its order reversal.\nL113: Each event combines one of six leg locations with one of four signal kinds and has binary strength, duration, and speed attributes. The 24 location–kind channels drive disjoint groups of anatomically annotated MaleCNS leg afferents, comprising 2,837 neurons. Event attributes determine the amplitudes and timing of the sensory input. Answers are rendered from a 100-word lexicon using authored task conventions, while the model vocabulary also includes three punctuation marks and six control symbols.\nL114: Two fixed $128\\times 109$ codebooks assign orthonormal input and output codes to these 109 symbols.\nL115: Let $a_{t}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{24}$ contain the task-defined sensory amplitudes and let $q_{t}$ be the one-hot indicator for the current symbol. With fixed sensory and token gains $g_{s}$ and $g_{w}$, the language drive is\nL116: \nL117:  | $$u_{t}=g_{s}P_{s}a_{t}+g_{w}P_{w}E_{\\mathrm{in}}q_{t}.$$  |  | (2)\nL118: For sensory group $S_{g}$, $(P_{s})_{ig}=|S_{g}|^{-1/2}$ when $i\\in S_{g}$ and zero otherwise, while each code dimension is copied by $P_{w}$ to eight token-input nodes. Token nodes are sampled without replacement from nodes with outgoing edges after excluding sensory nodes, and eight output nodes per code dimension are sampled from nodes with incoming edges after excluding both earlier sets. The sensory, token-input, and output sets are therefore disjoint.\nL119: The four interfaces reuse the sensory groups and codebooks but place token and output nodes differently.\nL120: If $O_{d}$ is the eight-node output group for code dimension $d$, the readout is\nL121: \nL122:  | $$z_{t,d}=\\frac{1}{8}\\sum_{i\\in O_{d}}h_{t,i},\\qquad\\ell_{t}=\\frac{E_{\\mathrm{out}}^{\\top}z_{t}}{\\tau\\max(\\lVert z_{t}\\rVert_{2},10^{-6})}.$$  |  | (3)\nL123: \nL124: Here $\\ell_{t}$ contains the symbol logits and $\\tau$ is the temperature.\nL185: ## AI Use Statement\nL186: \nL187: We used OpenAI generative AI tools to develop and review the synthetic data generator and to polish the manuscript’s prose. Their use for the training code, equations, literature, methodology, result interpretation, figures, and translations was limited to review. The authors take responsibility for the final content.\nL188: ## References\nL189:   * Berg et al. (2026) Berg, S., et al. (2026). Sexual dimorphism in the complete Drosophila male central nervous system connectome. Cell, 189:5504–5526.e15.\nL190:   * Dhiman (2026) Dhiman, N. (2026). Topological sensitivity in connectome-constrained neural networks. arXiv preprint arXiv:2604.04033.\nL191:   * Dorkenwald et al. (2024) Dorkenwald, S., Matsliah, A., Sterling, A. R., et al. (2024). Neuronal wiring diagram of an adult brain. Nature, 634:124–138.\nL192:   * Jin et al. (2026) Jin, Z., Zhu, Y., Zhang, C., and Sui, Y. (2026). Whole-brain connectomic graph model enables whole-body locomotion control in fruit fly. arXiv preprint arXiv:2602.17997.\nL193:   * Kim and Linzen (2020) Kim, N. and Linzen, T. (2020). COGS: A compositional generalization challenge based on semantic interpretation. In Proceedings of EMNLP.\nL194:   * Lappalainen et al. (2024) Lappalainen, J. K., Tschopp, F. D., Prakhya, S., et al. (2024). Connectome-constrained networks predict neural activity across the fly visual system. Nature, 634:1132–1140.\nL195:   * Liang et al. (2021) Liang, Y., Ryali, C. K., Hoover, B., Grinberg, L., Navlakha, S., Zaki, M. J., and Krotov, D. (2021). Can a fruit fly learn word embeddings? In International Conference on Learning Representations.\nL196:   * Lin et al. (2024) Lin, A., Yang, R., Dorkenwald, S., et al. (2024). Network statistics of the whole-brain connectome of Drosophila. Nature, 634:153–165.\nL197:   * McAllister et al. (2026) McAllister, J., Houghton, C., Wade, J., and O’Donnell, C. (2026). Non-random brain connectome wiring enables robust and efficient neural network function under high sparsity. bioRxiv preprint 2026.03.30.715411.\nL198:   * Morra et al. (2025) Morra, J., Fouke, K., Naumann, E. A., and Daley, M. (2025). Connectomes inform function: from time-varying dynamics to animal behaviour. Natural Computing, 24(3):511–528.\nL199:   * Nikankin et al. (2025) Nikankin, Y., Reusch, A., Mueller, A., and Belinkov, Y. (2025). Arithmetic without algorithms: Language models solve math with a bag of heuristics. In International Conference on Learning Representations.\nL200:   * Pircher et al. (2021) Pircher, T., Pircher, B., Schlücker, E., and Feigenspan, A. (2021). The structure dilemma in biological and artificial neural networks. Scientific Reports, 11:5621.\nL201:   * Pospisil et al. (2024) Pospisil, D. A., Aragon, M. J., Dorkenwald, S., et al. (2024). The fly connectome reveals a path to the effectome. Nature, 634:201–209.\nL202:   * Riveland and Pouget (2024) Riveland, R. and Pouget, A. (2024). Natural language instructions induce compositional generalization in networks of neurons. Nature Neuroscience, 27:988–999.\nL203:   * Ruis et al. (2020) Ruis, L., Andreas, J., Baroni, M., Bouchacourt, D., and Lake, B. M. (2020). A benchmark for systematic generalization in grounded language understanding. In Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems.\nL204:   * Shiu et al. (2024) Shiu, P. K., Sterne, G. R., Spiller, N., et al. (2024). A Drosophila computational brain model reveals sensorimotor processing. Nature, 634:210–219.\nL205:   * Thrush et al. (2022) Thrush, T., Jiang, R., Bartolo, M., et al. (2022). Winoground: Probing vision and language models for visio-linguistic compositionality. In Proceedings of CVPR.\nL206:   * Vong et al. (2024) Vong, W. K., Wang, W., Orhan, A. E., and Lake, B. M. (2024). Grounded language acquisition through the eyes and ears of a single child. Science, 383:504–511.\nL207:   * Wang and Chen (2026) Wang, B. and Chen, J. (2026). FLYNN: Robust neural network for robot navigation using fly brain topology. arXiv preprint arXiv:2607.00025.\nL208:   * Wang et al. (2026) Wang, C., Dong, X., Ji, Z., Xiao, M., Jiang, J., Liu, X., Huan, Y., and Wu, S. (2026). Model-agnostic linear-memory online learning in spiking neural networks. Nature Communications, 17:1745.\nL209:   * Wu et al. (2021) Wu, Z., Kreiss, E., Ong, D. C., and Potts, C. (2021). ReaSCAN: Compositional reasoning in language grounding. In Proceedings of the Neural Information Processing Systems Track on Datasets and Benchmarks.\nL210:   * Yu et al. (2026) Yu, S., Qin, Z., Liu, T., Xu, B., Vogelstein, R. J., Brown, J., and Vogelstein, J. T. (2026). Biological processing units: Leveraging an insect connectome to pioneer biofidelic neural architectures. In Artificial General Intelligence: 18th International Conference, AGI 2025, Lecture Notes in Computer Science, vol. 16058, pp. 361–369.\nL211:   * Zhang et al. (2026) Zhang, X., Yang, P., Feng, J., Wen, K., Yan, G., Luo, Q., Lin, W., and Lu, X. (2026). Network structure governs Drosophila brain functionality. Fundamental Research, 6(3):1859–1868.\nL212: Supplementary Materials\nL213: \nL214: A Drosophila Whole-Connectome Network Can Learn Human-Designed Cognitive Tasks\nL215: ## Appendix A Data and Graph Provenance\nL216: \nL217: MaleCNS v1.0 reconstructs the CNS of a single adult male and was obtained from the official release page (【44†Berg et al., 2026】). The MaleCNS v1.0 dataset is available under CC BY 4.0.\nL218: We selected annotation rows with an assigned superclass, sorted retained body IDs, and kept a directed connection only when both endpoints survived selection. Repeated records for the same ordered neuron pair were aggregated by summing their contact counts. Contact counts define edge presence rather than initial weight magnitudes. Our rule retains 166,700 unique body IDs, matching the total reported by 【44†Berg et al. (2026)】.\nL219: Their connectivity graph contains 166,483 neurons after excluding 217 neurons without synaptic connections. Table 【78†2】 summarizes the source and processed graph counts.\nL220: Table 2: Processed graph and source statistics.\nL221: Quantity  | Value\nL222: Source-article neuron total  | 166,700 neurons\nL223: Source-article connected neurons  | 166,483 neurons\nL224: Selection  | Superclass annotation assigned\nL225: Selected neurons in processed graph  | 166,700\nL226: Directed aggregated edges  | 25,582,938\nL227: Synaptic contacts represented  | 124,177,617\nL228: Self-edges  | 101\nL229: Edge threshold  | None\nL230: Orientation  | postsynaptic rows, presynaptic columns\nL231: Graph orientation follows the implemented sparse matrix: rows are postsynaptic neurons and columns are presynaptic neurons. Thus a stored edge $(i,j)$ contributes from neuron $j$ to neuron $i$. The graph contains 101 self-edges, which remain unchanged under rewiring. Appendix 【36†D】 gives the rewire algorithm and structural diagnostics.\nL232: The anatomical rendering in Figure 【54†1】 uses a deterministic sample of 3,000 retained neurons with nonnull soma locations from the official precomputed skeleton service. The overlaid nodes and arrows illustrate the network schematically.\nL233: ## Appendix B Task, Model, and Training Details\nL234: \nL235: ### B.1 Shared recurrent parameterization\nL236: \nL237: In both tasks, the edge scalars are initialized independently from $\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ using a run-specific random state. The factor $1/\\sqrt{\\max(d_{i}^{\\mathrm{in}},1)}$ normalizes every edge entering node $i$, and recurrent states begin at zero. Input groups, output groups, graph indices and language codebooks are fixed, so only the edge scalars are optimized.\nL238: ### B.2 Addition task and optimization\nL239: \nL240: Input groups contain 64 graph nodes per channel, and output groups contain 32 nodes per class. The groups are sampled without replacement from nodes with outgoing or incoming edges and are disjoint. For an encoded expression $x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{47}$, channel $x_{c}$ is copied with amplitude 1 to every node in its input group at each recurrent update. If $R_{k}$ is the output group for answer $k$, its logit is\nL241:  | $$\\ell_{k}=\\frac{1}{4\\tau}\\sum_{t=T-3}^{T}\\frac{1}{|R_{k}|}\\sum_{i\\in R_{k}}h_{t,i},\\qquad T=64,$$  |\nL242: \nL243: with temperature $\\tau=0.1$. The readout has no learned weight or bias.\nL244: We use $\\alpha=0.5$, unit recurrent gain, and AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$ and weight decay $10^{-4}$. The gradient norm is clipped at 1, and the microbatch size is 128. Runs train for at most 100 epochs, with a minimum of 20 epochs and patience 15. If $p=\\operatorname{softmax}(\\ell)$ and $y\\in\\{0,\\ldots,100\\}$, the training loss is\nL245: \nL246:  | $$\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{add}}=-\\log p_{y}+\\frac{1}{100}\\sum_{c=0}^{99}\\left(\\sum_{k=0}^{c}p_{k}-\\mathbf{1}\\{y\\leq c\\}\\right)^{2}.$$  |\nL247: The second term penalizes disagreement between the predicted and target cumulative distributions over the ordered answer classes.\nL248: ### B.3 Language task specification\nL249: An event is a tuple $e=(l,k,v,d,s)$. Here $l$ is one of six leg locations, $k$ is one of four signal kinds, and $v$, $d$, and $s$ are binary strength, duration, and speed attributes. The channel index is $4l+k$, giving 24 sensory channels. Complete report answers describe all five attributes. Table 【68†3】 gives a held-out composition-test record.\nL250: The primary paired endpoint renders both descriptions in a fixed relation-first form (“$X$ happened before $Y$”) and leaves the question unchanged after reversing event order.\nL251: Table 3: Worked example of a paired language group.\nL252: Component  | Content\nL253: --- | ---\nL254: Question  | What did I feel?\nL255: Original scene $r$  | strong, long, slow pressure on the right middle leg; then weak, short, slow movement on the right front leg\nL256: Candidate $y$  | The strong pressure on the right middle leg happened before the weak movement on the right front leg. The first signal started slowly and stopped after a long time. The last signal started slowly and stopped after a short time.\nL257: Reversed scene $r^{\\leftrightarrow}$  | weak, short, slow movement on the right front leg; then strong, long, slow pressure on the right middle leg\nL258: Candidate $y^{\\leftrightarrow}$  | The weak movement on the right front leg happened before the strong pressure on the right middle leg. The first signal started slowly and stopped after a short time. The last signal started slowly and stopped after a long time.\nL259: Table 【79†4】 lists the additional meaning and action conventions used by a subset of question-answer and text families.\nL260: \nL261: Table 4: Authored signal conventions in the language generator.\nL262: Signal kind  | Rendered meaning  | Rendered action\nL263: --- | --- | ---\nL264: touch  | a signal from the body  | follow the signal\nL265: taste  | food  | approach the food\nL266: movement  | a change in the ground  | follow the movement\nL267: pressure  | pain and danger  | avoid that place\nL268: The complete lexical vocabulary is shown in Table 【80†5】. Punctuation and the six control symbols are separate model symbols and are not counted among the 100 words. The input and output codebooks have orthonormal columns and are generated once for use at all four interface placements. Sensory waveforms are deterministic functions of the split index and record identifier.\nL269: Table 5: The 100-word lexicon.\nL270: Category  | Words\nL271: --- | ---\nL272: Nouns  | fly, body, leg, legs, side, strength, touch, taste, movement, pressure, signal, sensation, event, events, pain, food, ground, danger, place, time, pulse, change\nL273: Attributes  | left, right, front, middle, hind, weak, strong, short, long, quickly, slowly, first, last, same, different, one, two, three, four\nL274: Verbs  | feel, felt, detect, detected, happen, happened, start, started, stop, stopped, mean, meant, avoid, avoided, approach, approached, follow, followed, do\nL275: Function words  | a, the, that, my, its, it, i, they, both, another, is, are, was, were, did, in, on, to, from, of, and, but, so, because, before, after, while, then, not, no, yes, what, where, when, which, why, how, many, there, or\nL276: The generator contains the families summarized in Table 【81†6】. Report families express complete event descriptions and supply the composition endpoint. Question-answer families isolate attributes, comparisons, temporal order, meanings, and actions. Text families provide next-symbol training on short controlled documents.\nL277: Table 6: Template families used by the deterministic corpus generator.\nL278: Mode  | Count  | Content\nL279: --- | --- | ---\nL280: Report  | 2  | First- and third-person descriptions of complete event sequences\nL281: Question answering  | 18  | Event identity and attributes, temporal order, location, comparisons, counts, meaning, action, and causal explanation\nL282: Controlled text  | 11  | Temporal and causal statements, contrasts, simultaneous events, counting, side descriptions, pulses, and short dialogue\nL283: ### B.4 Corpus generation and split construction\nL284: The generator indexes the 24 sensory channels from 0 to 23. It assigns the 24 adjacent cyclic pairs $\\{i,(i+1)\\bmod 24\\}$ to composition validation and the 24 offset-five pairs $\\{i,(i+5)\\bmod 24\\}$ to composition test after canonicalizing pair order, leaving the remaining 228 unordered pairs for training. Scenes are sampled before wording is rendered, so every event order and paraphrase inherits the same pair partition.\nL285: Familiar-family validation uses a deterministic hash reservation over attributed scenes and additionally reserves prompt identities for text records.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn4view4 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/nn.4401\",\"pattern\":\"Abstract\"}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.nature.com/articles/nn.4401: (500) Redirected to https://idp.nature.com/authorize?response_type=cookie&client_id=grover&redirect_uri=https://www.nature.com/articles/nn.4401 which is not accessible via this tool. (non-retryable error)"
